<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giuseppe Mazzini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giuseppe Mazzini was a Genoese revolutionary and founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a>, who campaigned for a united, republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 1848 he joined the republicans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and in 1849 he led the triumvirate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> until French troops restored papal rule. He spent most of his life in exile, and Italy was united as a monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Mazzini" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giuseppe_Mazzini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mazzini was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, which had recently been annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">First French Empire</a>. His father Giacomo was a university professor who had adhered to Jacobin ideology, and his mother Maria Drago was known for her Jansenist religious fervour. He entered university at 14 and graduated in law in 1826, practising at first as a poor man's lawyer, while hoping to become a historical novelist or dramatist. In 1827 he travelled to Tuscany and joined the Carbonari. He was arrested at Genoa on 31 October 1830, and on his release in early 1831 he chose exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> rather than confinement in a small hamlet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In exile he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> with the aim of One, free, independent, republican nation and the motto God and the People. It counted about 60,000 adherents in 1833. The insurrection he planned that year was discovered before it began; twelve participants were executed, his friend Jacopo Ruffini killed himself, and Mazzini was sentenced to death in absentia. A second attempt in 1834 was crushed by Piedmontese troops. That year he also helped found Young Europe, hoping that free nations might one day form a loosely federal Europe. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in January 1837, in very poor circumstances.</p><p>On 7 April 1848 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, but he had never been popular there because he wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to become a republic rather than join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, and he left after the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-italian-war-of-independence">First Italian War of Independence</a>. A republic was declared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 9 February 1849, and Mazzini arrived the same day. On 29 March he became one of its triumvirate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-armellini">Carlo Armellini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurelio-saffi">Aurelio Saffi</a>, and soon its true leader. French troops called by the Pope defeated the republican troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>, and he left on 12 July 1849. Failed risings in Mantua in 1852 and Milan in 1853 damaged his movement's prestige.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1860 he published Doveri dell'uomo, Duties of Man, which argued that individual rights are won through work, sacrifice and virtue and called for recognition of women's rights. He joined faith to politics, describing himself as a Christian and a deist, and he held that thought must be followed by action. He opposed Marxism and class struggle. Critics have argued that his refusal to compromise with the monarchy made his goals harder to reach. He refused a seat in the Italian Chamber of Deputies in 1867 and died of pleurisy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> on 10 March 1872, aged 66. About 100,000 people took part in his funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giuseppe Mazzini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the partitions, Polish poets and musicians kept alive a national culture without a state, and <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b>, who founded Young Italy in 1831, preached the brotherhood of free nations.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b> revived the republican idea in Italy, Brazil’s radical liberals founded a Republican Party in 1870, and Spain had two republics, in 1873–1874 and 1931–1939.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Moderates led by Casati favoured union with Piedmont, while republicans such as Cattaneo and <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b> wanted to delay the question.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-milan">Provisional Government of Milan</a></li><li><blockquote>A triumvirate of <b>Giuseppe Mazzini</b>, Aurelio Saffi and Carlo Armellini governed.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-republic-1849">Roman Republic (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giuseppe Mazzini"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Be your country your Temple: God at the summit; a people of equals at the base.&#8221;</p><footer>The Duties of Man, ch. 5 (1858) &#183; English translation published 1898 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://history.hanover.edu/texts/mazzini/mazzini5.html" rel="noopener">Hanover Historical Texts Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Say not I, but We. Let each man among you strive to incarnate his country in himself.&#8221;</p><footer>The Duties of Man, ch. 5 (1858) &#183; English translation published 1898 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://history.hanover.edu/texts/mazzini/mazzini5.html" rel="noopener">Hanover Historical Texts Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The Emancipation of Woman, then, must be regarded by you as necessarily linked with the emancipation of the workingman.&#8221;</p><footer>The Duties of Man, conclusion (1858) &#183; English translation published 1898 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://history.hanover.edu/texts/mazzini/mazini12.html" rel="noopener">Hanover Historical Texts Project</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Love your country. It is your name, your glory, your sign among the peoples. Give to it your thought, your counsel, your blood.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Young Men of Italy, speech in memory of the Bandiera brothers, 1848 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_the_Young_Men_of_Italy" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…love and reverence the Ideal; that is the country of the spirit, the city of the soul, in which all are brethren who believe in the inviolability of thought, and in the dignity of our immortal natures.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Young Men of Italy, speech in memory of the Bandiera brothers, 1848 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_the_Young_Men_of_Italy" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Love humanity. You can only ascertain your own mission from the aim placed by God before humanity at large.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Young Men of Italy, speech in memory of the Bandiera brothers, 1848 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_the_Young_Men_of_Italy" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
